bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/research/face-intent-plane-2026.mem:30:# ''The field splits what the daemon doc fused'' (both waves, MEASURED). The roster (Keyhive: a person as a group of device keys; Keybase's sigchain); shared state; per-device delivery (Matrix to-device, its transaction id scoped to one device and one endpoint); the signed request (a UCAN invocation, its task id a content hash of subject, command, arguments and nonce); the outcome (a receipt whose `cause` names the request; Temporal's recorded results); the authority (a delegation chain whose revocations append and arrive out of order). Two answerers for one summons follows from one mutable entry holding request and answer.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/canvas-field-scout.mem:702:The specific and checkable overlap is **Keyhive**: they are building, in the open, exactly the offline-capable capability layer your face-join and plane laws describe, and their *"least surprise" principle for concurrent permission changes* is a live design question on which you have independently ruled. **The pragmatic reading: you are not positioned to lead here. You are positioned to be the first real deployment of what they publish** — and to feed back the one thing a research lab structurally cannot generate, which is a house that has to actually run every day.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-d-lane.mem:40:# ''The seat lands when the grant arrives'': one serialized seat act owns the later grant's take, verify, ingest and the floor lift, with one in-flight promise, so duplicate wakes coalesce and a wake that lands mid-act earns one trailing pass. Three causal wakes feed it and nothing else: a change on the retained PersonaGroup document, filtered to this vessel's `face-join-grant/v1` title; a daemon-layer change under the group's lease prefix, and, only while a grant waits unjudged on an unavailable lease frontier, any daemon-layer change (a late layer wakes it even when it holds no lease slot, since the composite forwards a joining layer's records as changes); and one replay at boot once the lease listener attaches. The PersonaGroup document listener attaches when that plane arrives and replays the act once, so a boot never waits on an unsynced plane and no grant falls between the boot pass and the listener. A signature reads judged only after its ingest lands (a definitive verify refusal judges once per boot); an ingest fault, or a lease frontier that reads unavailable, leaves the record unjudged for the next causal change. No clock, no poll, no retry timer. `faceSeated` reports `knowsAgent` and takes nothing; vessel-only reuse reads the record and returns it; the mint path waits for the seat act before it reads the seat. The binding rests in D, in the face subtree, never in the floor index. Reds: a grant arriving after a vessel-only binding seats and lifts with no binding call (control: no grant, the floor stays); a grant before the daemon layer stays unjudged until the layer's join seats it; duplicate pulses ingest once; an ingest fault then a later change still seats; a crash between ingest and lift finishes once at restart.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/crdt-ecosystem-scout.mem:164:1. **Do not ship on `@keyhive/keyhive@0.1.0-alpha.8` as a security boundary.** PR #216 (2026-08-27) fixes transitive authority exceeding the weakest link on the best path, and **Relay access to a group transitively granting CGKA-tree membership in a document** — with tests that fail on the pre-fix code. It landed *after* alpha.8 and is unreleased. This lands squarely on our `#has`/Relay cap-tier model and the face-join plane law. Track for alpha.9 or build from `main`.
