bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-face-join-reseal.mem:34:The hazard — ''"face-join never re-grants bindings minted vessel-only before the seat"'' — was real, but the measured root cause is deeper than the held canon recorded. I measured it against the ''real'' `KeyhiveProvider` (single booted provider reused — no second holder) and refined the ruling.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-face-join-reseal.mem:44:So the committed re-grant loop ''overclaimed'': `regranted` counted the re-pointed delegation, but keyhive reads FORWARD-ONLY — a re-delegate re-keys the bag forward and moves no already-sealed chunk. The codebase already knew this in `persona-crossing.ts` line 105: ''"route A owes a re-encrypt."'' face-join owed it and never paid it.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-face-join-reseal.mem:49:# ''`face-join.ts`'' — the re-grant loop, after each `delegate`, calls the optional `provider.reSealBag`; the grant now carries a `reSealed` field (the fresh chunks, base64) so the joinee ingests reachable ciphertext. The `regranted` doc comment is corrected to state the invariant: a re-delegate confers FORWARD reach only; pre-seat chunks need `reSealed`.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-face-join-reseal.mem:50:# ''`operator-daemon-behavior.ts`'' (face-join grant path only) — the daemon `joinProvider` wires `reSealBag` to the vessel provider (the holder that sealed the content and holds bag admin).
