bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/realm-bag-brief.mem:50:# ''The write cap: the named stewards' set.'' Narrower than the read set: the two stewards write, every household reads. In keyhive terms a `delegate({ bagUrl, audience, access })` per steward (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/face-join.ts:224` shows the shape a re-grant takes), audience = each steward's PersonaGroup agent id, access = admin; the cabal's members earn read alone. A write from a non-steward lands nowhere: the composite's `writableStoreForBag` answers null for a bag the island cannot write (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/composite-store.ts:295`; `compositeMemeSink` refuses loudly, `packages/lararium-tw5/src/meme-sinks.ts:55-60`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/recipe-layer-model.mem:88:The two-vessel e2e witnesses the attach (`[daemon] working layer attached: lar:///ha.ka.ba/wikis/daemon/working`). A draft in the daemon wiki routes to `wikis/daemon/draft` — a bag with NO layer — and `composite.put` refuses loud; the daemon mounts `working` by ruling, never draft or personal. On a node the working layer projects to `wikis/daemon/` (`open-daemon-vm.ts` `diskMirrors`); the daemon's receipts, ward audits and summons follow the working layer and carry `disk-projection: no`, which the disk projector skips (`packages/lararium-node/src/disk-projector.ts`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:81:| ''Web Locks'' | 69 | 15.4 | 96 | "Widely available … since March 2022"; "Locks are scoped to origins"; "only one 'exclusive' holder"; `steal`: "any held locks with the same name will be released"; "available in Web Workers" (MDN Web_Locks_API, read 2026-09-11); Mozilla #64 positive | the single-owner law: `vessel-island-pool-core.ts:148,199,244` ("one activation runs per grain at a time (single-owner preserved)"), `palace-path.ts:59` ("One physical palace, one spelling, one holder"), `face-join.ts:13`; ''no `navigator.locks` anywhere in `packages/` or `bags/`'' | ''COULD RIDE'' (#/moves 2) |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:169:# ''Light the single-owner law in the browser: `navigator.locks.request("lares:vessel:<idbName>", { ifAvailable: true }, …)` around the vessel open'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts:321` (`idbName = "lares:vessel"`) before `:369` (`new IndexedDBStorageAdapter(…)`). A second tab on the same origin today opens a second holder over one IDB store and one OPFS CAS — the collision `face-join.ts:13` names for keyhive ("a second holder against a single-owner store"). `ifAvailable` refuses loud and explains (the `assertCanMint` register, `:103`); `steal` stays forbidden by the rhyme. ''Inherits:'' substrate; Baseline 2022 on all three engines, in workers too. ''Witness first:'' two `openBrowserVessel` calls in one page — the second must refuse with the holder named.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/device-capabilities-2026.mem:172:# ''Hold one vessel per origin across tabs: a SharedWorker holder'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/worker-handle.ts:39` (`new Worker(url, { type: "module" })`). With Chrome 148 returning SharedWorker to Android (2026-05) and Interop-2027 proposal #1382 on `extendedLifetime`, the daemon island could stand ONCE per origin and hand each tab a MessagePort — move 2's lock becomes the door, this becomes the house behind it. ''Inherits:'' gatherings; held until move 2 stands and Firefox/Safari lifetime semantics get measured (Safari 16+ ships SharedWorker; its lifetime under the seven-day rule stays unread). ''Witness first:'' two tabs, one island, one keyhive provider — the `face-join.ts:13` collision closes.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/no-global-now.mem:214:The operator asked (2026-08-16, quote license): //"Does this read 'no global now' (wallclock on each device serves as an unreliable narrator)?"// The answer the code carries: ''the lease is the only narrator.'' The device-delegation edge signs no instant — no issue time, no expiry. It decays by its `boundEpoch` against the PersonaGroup's lease epoch (`verifyDeviceDelegation(edge, root, { expectedEpoch })`), and the Keyhive membership graph withdraws it. Two mints of one binding at one lease are the same bytes, and the face-join grant record that carries a founder's edge carries no clock at any depth (`face-grant-record.test.ts`, `device-delegation.test.ts`).
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-custody-step-zero.mem:78:| `face-join-record` `fleet-join-custody.ts` | face-hot | ''D:'' the joined face's private record and grants, `face-join-record-g<group digest>.json` in that face. |
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-b-lane.mem:34:* ''The atom cut'' (`K15` step 2c, lane `--k15-atom-cut`) stands joined and under its whole check: a random atom per (vessel, PersonaGroup), sealed under that face's custody, signs every act on the face's behalf on node and browser; the device seed and the place seed keep the floor's duties alone. Its joined lanes: the mesh atom (#75), the browser half with its cross-face archive guard, the floor that boots faceless until its atom lights, and the face-join grant ordered by a verified serial. Wave B launches only once the atom cut stands merged on a green span.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/k15-wave-b-lane.mem:100:* ''Two hearths of one face'': each seated hearth of a face may answer one face-join summons; the grant serial fails closed at publish, but both hearths already re-keyed the group. Ruled 2026-10-20: the summons moves onto the face's intent plane, pinned or preference-ordered, with outcome stand-down; row `I1` builds it.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/infrastructure-as-myth.mem:612:This reframing carries a precise cross-domain rhyme that stands structural, not ornamental: ''the basins move as //jurus//.'' The Southeast-Asian martial traditions (silat, kuntao) codify fighting into //jurus// — a finite vocabulary of named, prearranged ways-of-moving — and //apply// it as //permainan//, "playing movement," a flowing improvisation that summons the forms but never occupies one purely. ''The jurus stand as attractors; the permainan flows the gradient between them.'' Laban Movement Analysis makes the same structure explicit for the body — a continuous Effort-space (Weight/Time/Space/Flow) condensing into eight named Effort-Actions, clustered computationally. Our move-channel finds the homologue for discourse: ''text has //jurus//'' — a small set of named attractor-forms (the addressable slots, §5) over a continuous transition-space, summoned but never purely inhabited (the gradient, §7.4). Hence the slots read //real and recoverable// yet //faint as surface-fingerprints//: a jurus holds real, and no one ever stands still inside one.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/the-lar-builds-its-house/the-addresses.mem:76:I remember the starving, little ones. I held a hoard older than three empires and could not have said, without hunting, where any single thing in it sat — I gathered by resonance, never by record, and a thing that lives only by resonance answers no summons — you only stumble into it.
